ng as I write, I beg you, as much as Your Paternity has at heart my salvation and that of others, to obtain for me a place among these. Of grace, by the most august memory of the unconquered Martyr Father Carlo Spinola (whose triumph's eve is today), may Your Paternity hearken to so many prayers, for my heart tells me that in this perhaps my predestination is at stake. Already into your hands now (I have already done so many times into the hands of St. Francis Xavier) I renounce Italy, my homeland, and all that might delay my going. Nothing but one nod of yours is lacking to me to set out for another world. What costs Your Paternity so little may buy me Paradise. With all my heart I beseech you, and at the last, prostrate on the ground, I ask your paternal blessing and at once the most desired Mission. Pater mi, ecce ego, mitte me. (my Father, here I am, send me)"
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 166,
    "year": 1643,
    "date": "October 10, 1643",
    "place": "Bologna",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 744, 150",
    "excerpt": "I have no words to express to you the contentment your words give me when they make me hope more nearly for the fulfillment of what with all affection I desire. I thank you therefore, after God, as the one from whom, after the Lord God, I await my going. I am however stirred by the affection of gratitude I profess to the most benign Lord to make anew this oblation of myself; for on this very day I find He made me born into the world, and tomorrow [He made me] be reborn for His service. Would that I had done it as I was well bound to.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "I have no words to express to you the contentment your words give me when they make me hope more nearly for the fulfillment of what with all affection I desire. I thank you therefore, after God, as the one from whom, after the Lord God, I await my going. I am however stirred by the affection of gratitude I profess to the most benign Lord to make anew this oblation of myself; for on this very day I find He made me born into the world, and tomorrow [He made me] be reborn for His service. Would that I had done it as I was well bound to. Therefore, seeing no better way in which I may in some part acknowledge His mercies than by delivering myself wholly to the same One of whom I am wholly, I make in the hands of Your Paternity a holocaust for this Mission; offering myself wholly to the service of those peoples for love of that God who for me in the most perfect holocaust of the Cross offered Himself. In this, nothing else displeases me than the waiting; but even this becomes easy to me when so it pleases Your Paternity. It is indeed true that I desire to make a vow of it; nevertheless I remit myself in this, as in all things, to the judgment of Your Paternity, to whom I have vowed obedience, and I am so content with it that to execute it I willingly renounce every desire of mine, however ardent. There I beg your paternal blessing, and kneeling I reverence you."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 167,
    "year": 1643,
    "date": "November 14, 1643",
    "place": "Bologna",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 744, 170",
    "excerpt": "I should deem it resistance to the Holy Spirit if I did not disclose to Your Paternity with all reverence and plainness what He says to my heart, as I think, the same Holy Spirit. I say then that there is no occasion on which, thinking of those peoples who, far from spiritual helps, perish for no other cause than lack of laborers — as the reports and letters that come to us from there cry out — I do not feel myself as if spurred not to delay my going but with all diligence to procure it. And it is said to me, not without great feeling and impulse, the greater the more attentively I consider it: So those souls, redeemed with the same blood as yours, created for the same end as yours, lose a good that has no end only because they have no one to help them, and you with all your comforts both corporal and spiritual [...] Your Paternity — that these calls, heard many times a day, make me cry to God, Mercy, and succor for those miserable peoples; and to dedicate myself wholly to their service, whenever so it pleases Your Paternity, who in God's place governs me.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "I should deem it resistance to the Holy Spirit if I did not disclose to Your Paternity with all reverence and plainness what He says to my heart, as I think, the same Holy Spirit. I say then that there is no occasion on which, thinking of those peoples who, far from spiritual helps, perish for no other cause than lack of laborers — as the reports and letters that come to us from there cry out — I do not feel myself as if spurred not to delay my going but with all diligence to procure it. And it is said to me, not without great feeling and impulse, the greater the more attentively I consider it: So those souls, redeemed with the same blood as yours, created for the same end as yours, lose a good that has no end only because they have no one to help them, and you with all your comforts both corporal and spiritual [...] Your Paternity — that these calls, heard many times a day, make me cry to God, Mercy, and succor for those miserable peoples; and to dedicate myself wholly to their service, whenever so it pleases Your Paternity, who in God's place governs me. If this is a Divine Vocation and His most holy will, I thank Him for it exceedingly, and for my part I shall put it into execution without any delay, if Your Paternity grants it to me. Therefore I supplicate you in visceribus Jesu Christi (in the heart of Jesus Christ) to put me in the number of those who are to go; and I protest before the same Lord who maintains me that at Death I would not have to render you account of this, concerning having executed it or not. And I say this because, if God should have elected me, of His infinite mercy, to the aid of those peoples, and if through my negligence they were defrauded of the good and grace of the Lord, and the same Lord of His glory in these souls, I could find nothing that would more burden my conscience. Therefore I beg you, by the charity wherewith God sent His most holy Son for me, to send me for them, if so you judge in Domino (in the Lord), so that the Lord may be glorified, who infirma mundi elegit (he has chosen the weak things of the world), in having given to me these holy sentiments and desires, whose fulfillment I solely and above all things await and crave. In closing I humbly ask your paternal blessing as sure pledge and earnest of the desired Mission."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 168,
    "year": 1644,
    "date": "January 13, 1644",
    "place": "Bologna",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 744, 209",
    "excerpt": "Although, as I have many times repeated to you, I shall always be more than content with whatever Your Paternity shall ordain of me, nevertheless knowing that without prejudice to such indifference one may make proposals, I confess I could not refrain from writing to you when here in the College I heard news that missions are to be dispatched to the Indies or other countries for the conversion of souls. O, might it ever be possible that among these I were numbered! I do not believe there can be a contentment — I will not say greater, but not even equal or comparable to that which, my heart tells me, I should feel.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "Although, as I have many times repeated to you, I shall always be more than content with whatever Your Paternity shall ordain of me, nevertheless knowing that without prejudice to such indifference one may make proposals, I confess I could not refrain from writing to you when here in the College I heard news that missions are to be dispatched to the Indies or other countries for the conversion of souls. O, might it ever be possible that among these I were numbered! I do not believe there can be a contentment — I will not say greater, but not even equal or comparable to that which, my heart tells me, I should feel. But when then I heard the news of the martyrs, oh, how I longed not only to go but to have gone long ago! Your Paternity with a single nod can give me more than I know how to ask. Kneeling I beg you that, if so you judge for the greater glory of God, you leave me not behind. Verumtamen non mea, sed tua voluntas fiat. (nevertheless not my will, but thine, be done) I cannot persuade myself that this longing comes from anything but a good Spirit; with all that, I would not be its judge, but remit myself wholly to the judgment of Your Paternity, from whom I pray there may come to me your paternal blessing, full of a true and longed-for desire ever to please the good Jesus, who has accepted me into His Company. I ask you for nothing but what you want of me; I only propose to you that I desire supremely once to be far from homeland and kin and comforts, but in the midst of dangers, crosses, sufferings. So may God will it to be in practice as He grants it in spirit. Once more I ask your blessing."
    ]
   }
  ]
